          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The answer is not the same for every house, and the variables are not subtle. Floor type, clutter, pets and whether the dock empties itself decide almost entirely whether a robot vacuum becomes something you would not live without or something you stop switching on by March.
        </p>

        <div id="the-honest-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Honest Answer Depends on Four Things
          </h2>

          <p>
            Robot vacuums are either one of the best appliance purchases available or an expensive gadget that ends up in a cupboard, and which of those you get is remarkably predictable in advance.
          </p>

          <p>
            It comes down to four characteristics of your home: what your floors are made of, how cluttered they are, whether you have pets or children, and whether the machine has a dock that empties itself. Get three or four of those in your favour and the robot becomes invisible infrastructure you would replace immediately if it broke. Get one or none and it is a machine you fight with.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single best predictor is floor type. On hard floors — tile, sealed hardwood, vinyl plank — a robot removes the daily dust and hair that otherwise accumulates visibly, and it does it well enough that the upright comes out far less often. On medium and high-pile carpet, it skims the surface and leaves the embedded grit, which is the part that actually matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second best predictor is clutter. A robot needs floor area it can traverse unattended. In a house where cables, shoes, toys and pet bowls are permanent floor features, a machine without excellent obstacle avoidance spends its run stuck, and the chore you removed is replaced by a different one.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Quick Test</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Hard floors, a pet, a tidy-ish floor plan and a self-emptying dock? Buy one.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Thick carpet throughout, a cluttered floor, several storeys and a tight budget? Put the money into a better conventional vacuum instead and revisit in a few years.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="what-you-get" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What You Actually Get for the Money
          </h2>

          <p>
            The benefit is not a cleaner house on any given day; it is a house that is consistently at the same level rather than cycling between clean and grubby.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dust and pet hair accumulate continuously. A weekly vacuum means the floor is excellent on Saturday afternoon and visibly deteriorating by Wednesday. A robot running daily holds the floor at a steady state, and the psychological effect of that is larger than people expect — the floor simply stops being something you notice or plan around.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second benefit is the removal of a recurring decision. In most households one person carries the mental load of noticing when floors need doing and either doing it or asking somebody else to. A scheduled robot removes that entirely, and for many families that is the part they would not give up.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is reach. A robot gets under beds, sofas and sideboards where an upright has never been, and in a house with a robot running daily those spaces simply stay clean rather than accumulating dust for years until a house move reveals it.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You Gain</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Realistic Value</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Daily floor maintenance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-20 minutes a day not spent vacuuming</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Consistent floor condition</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Floors always look recently cleaned</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under-furniture access</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Areas an upright never reaches</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Removal of a recurring chore decision</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Genuinely reduces household friction</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pet hair control</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The single strongest use case</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="what-it-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What It Really Costs Over Five Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            The purchase price is the smaller half of the picture once you count consumables and batteries.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Budget Model</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Premium Model</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Purchase</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$250-$500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$900-$1,800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Consumables per year</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$60-$120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$120-$200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery replacement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$50-$90 at 3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$80-$120 at 3-5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical lifespan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-7 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Five-year total</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-$1,100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,600-$3,000</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Consumables are the line people underestimate. Dock bags, filters, side brushes, main brushes and mop pads all wear out, and a busy house with pets goes through them faster than the manual suggests. Budget $100 to $200 a year for a premium machine in real use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set against that, the time saved is roughly ten to twenty minutes a day of floor maintenance you no longer do. Over five years that is several hundred hours, which makes even the expensive machines straightforward value for most people — provided the robot actually runs daily, which brings the conversation back to autonomy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest counter-argument is that a $250 robot that needs rescuing every run delivers none of that, and a $1,500 robot in a heavily carpeted house delivers little of it either. The money is only well spent when the house suits the machine.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="who-should-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Who Should Not Buy One
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is worth being direct about the households where a robot disappoints, because the marketing never is.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thick Carpet Throughout</h3>
          <p>
            Medium and high-pile carpet defeats most robots. Limited suction and a small brush cannot lift embedded grit, and some machines struggle to drive on deep pile at all. If every room is thickly carpeted, the money is better spent on a good upright with a powerful brush roll.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Genuinely Cluttered Floors</h3>
          <p>
            If the floor is a permanent landing zone for cables, shoes, laundry and toys, a robot either gets stuck constantly or requires a tidying pass before every run. Excellent obstacle avoidance helps enormously, but it is a premium feature and it does not cope with everything.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Multi-Storey Homes on a Budget</h3>
          <p>
            A robot cleans the floor it is on and its dock lives on that floor. Carrying it upstairs works but removes self-emptying and much of the convenience. Households that want robot coverage throughout a three-storey house usually end up buying two, which changes the economics considerably.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Homes With Very Dark Floors or Deep-Pile Dark Rugs</h3>
          <p>
            Cliff sensors read reflected infrared, and very dark or matte surfaces can absorb enough that the robot reads a drop and refuses to cross. It is a solvable annoyance — most manufacturers offer a setting or a strip of reflective tape as a workaround — but it surprises people, and it is worth knowing before you buy rather than after.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="what-decides-value" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Features That Decide Whether It Is Worth It
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are buying, these are the specifications that determine the value you get, roughly in order of importance.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Obstacle Avoidance</h3>
          <p>
            The strongest single predictor of whether a robot keeps being used. A machine that eats a charging cable once gets supervised thereafter, which defeats the purpose entirely. Premium LiDAR-plus-camera systems are meaningfully better, and this is the one place the money is clearly well spent.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Self-Emptying Dock</h3>
          <p>
            Without one you empty a small bin every two or three runs — a new chore in place of the old one. A dock that empties the robot into a sealed bag turns the machine into something you interact with monthly rather than weekly, and it is the upgrade most owners say they would not go back on.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brush Design</h3>
          <p>
            Rubber rollers and anti-tangle designs handle pet hair without the weekly ritual of cutting hair free with scissors. In a house with long-haired occupants or pets, this matters more than suction.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Body Height</h3>
          <p>
            Under three inches gets beneath most sofas and bed frames, which is where the dust an upright never reaches actually lives. Taller machines with elaborate turrets or climbing hardware do not, and that cuts out a meaningful share of the benefit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Suction, by contrast, matters less than the marketing implies — beyond roughly 5,000 Pa the difference on ordinary floors is hard to detect, and the other four features determine the experience far more. Our <a href="/best-robot-vacuum/">robot vacuum comparison</a> ranks machines on exactly these terms.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="living-with-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Living With One Is Actually Like
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two things surprise new owners, one good and one less so.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good one: within a fortnight the robot becomes invisible. It runs while you are out, the floors are simply always acceptable, and you stop thinking about the machine at all. That is the sign it was the right purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            The less good one: the maintenance is real, if small. Brushes need hair cut off them every week or two even on anti-tangle designs, the dust bin or dock bag needs attention, sensors need wiping, and mop pads need washing properly rather than relying on the dock. Skipping all of that produces a machine that gradually cleans worse and eventually smells, and the owner concludes the robot has failed when it has simply been neglected.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other adjustment is a habit rather than a chore: a brief floor tidy before the robot's scheduled run. In households where that becomes automatic, the robot almost never gets stuck. In households where it does not, obstacle avoidance has to carry all the weight, and even the best systems occasionally lose.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            A last practical note on resale and hand-me-downs: robot vacuums hold very little value second-hand, largely because the battery is a consumable and buyers know it. That makes trying one cheaply on the used market a reasonable experiment, and it makes upgrading an expensive one a poor financial move. Buy the machine you want to keep for five years rather than planning to trade up.
          </p>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a home with hard floors, a pet, and a floor plan that is broadly navigable, a robot vacuum with good obstacle avoidance and a self-emptying dock is one of the highest-value appliances you can buy. It removes a daily chore permanently, holds the floors at a consistent standard, and reaches places a conventional vacuum never has.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a home with thick carpet throughout, permanently cluttered floors, or several storeys and a tight budget, it is not, and the money is better spent on a genuinely good conventional vacuum plus a cordless stick for spot cleaning.
          </p>

          <p>
            The middle case — hard floors but a tight budget — is worth thinking about carefully. A cheap robot with poor obstacle avoidance and no self-emptying dock delivers a fraction of the benefit and is the version most likely to end up unused. If the budget only reaches the bottom of the market, waiting and saving usually produces a better outcome than buying now.
          </p>

          <p>
            And in every case, keep the conventional vacuum. A robot is a maintenance machine, not a deep-cleaning one, and stairs, upholstery and carpet still need the tool built for them — a point we set out in full in our <a href="/robot-vacuum-vs-regular-vacuum/">robot versus regular vacuum comparison</a>.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Cheapest Model to Try the Category</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bottom-of-market robots have poor obstacle avoidance and no self-emptying dock, which means frequent rescues and a bin to empty every second run. That version of the experience is why so many robots end up in cupboards, and it is a poor basis for judging whether the category suits you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying One for a Thickly Carpeted House</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Deep pile defeats the suction and agitation a robot can offer, and some machines cannot drive on it reliably. The value is concentrated on hard floors and low pile; on thick carpet the money belongs in a conventional vacuum with a powerful brush roll.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming It Replaces the Upright</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A robot cannot do stairs, upholstery or car interiors, and it does not deep clean carpet. Households that dispose of their conventional vacuum after buying a robot generally buy another one within a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Weekly Brush Clean</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even anti-tangle brushes collect hair around the bearings, and pickup declines gradually enough that owners blame the battery or the motor. Two minutes with the supplied cutting tool every week or two keeps the machine performing as it did when new.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It Manually Rather Than on a Schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The entire benefit comes from frequency, and frequency only happens automatically. A robot that has to be started by hand runs a couple of times a week and delivers a fraction of what it should.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are robot vacuums actually worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For homes with hard floors, pets and a navigable floor plan, yes — they remove ten to twenty minutes a day of floor maintenance and keep floors at a consistent standard rather than cycling between clean and grubby. For homes with thick carpet throughout or permanently cluttered floors, the value drops sharply.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot vacuums really save time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but less than the headline suggests unless the machine is autonomous. A robot with good obstacle avoidance and a self-emptying dock genuinely removes the daily pass. A cheap one that needs rescuing each run and has a bin you empty every second cycle replaces one chore with another.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a robot vacuum cost to own?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over five years, roughly $600 to $1,100 for a budget model and $1,600 to $3,000 for a premium one, including consumables at $60 to $200 a year and a battery replacement at the three to five year mark. Dock bags, filters, brushes and mop pads are the costs people underestimate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are expensive robot vacuums worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, because the premium buys the two features that decide whether the robot keeps being used: genuinely good obstacle avoidance and a dock that empties, washes and dries. Suction figures above about 5,000 Pa make little practical difference on ordinary floors, so do not pay for those.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot vacuums work on carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On low-pile carpet, yes — particularly models with rubber rollers. On medium and high-pile carpet they skim the surface and leave embedded grit, and some struggle to drive on deep pile at all. A thickly carpeted home is the clearest case for spending the money on a good upright instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a robot vacuum last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Three to five years for budget models and five to seven for premium ones, with the battery usually needing replacement at three to five years regardless. Brushes, filters and mop pads are consumables replaced far more often. Regular maintenance — cutting hair off brushes, wiping sensors — extends all of it noticeably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a robot vacuum work if my floors are cluttered?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only with excellent obstacle avoidance, and even then you will want a short tidying pass before scheduled runs. Cables, socks, toys and pet bowls are what stop robots, and a machine with basic bump sensing will get caught on all of them. If your floors are permanently busy, factor that into the budget.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

